% ============================================================================
% insect-parts — the three main body regions (tagmata) of an insect and what
% each one bears / is used for, as the source states it (adj-facts-stdlib,
% BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first big ideas of entomology, and a rung a BIOLOGY agent
% reasons UP from: an insect's body is divided into just THREE main regions —
% the head, the thorax, and the abdomen — and each region has a characteristic
% job. "The head carries the eyes, the antennae, and the mouthparts; the legs
% and wings attach at the thorax, which is the region for locomotion; the
% abdomen holds the main organs and is used for digestion and reproduction."
% Before you can reason about an insect's leg count, its wings, or its mouth,
% you must first know WHICH of the three regions the part belongs to. Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? insect_region_bears(thorax, $Bears)      % legs_and_wings
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `insect_region_bears(region, bears)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the value WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% a word that is not one of these three body regions (it never invents a part).
%
% Each of the three main body regions and what the source says it bears / is
% used for, as a little truth table:
%
%     body region   what it bears / is used for        a beginner's cue
%     -----------   -----------------------------      ------------------------
%     head          eyes_antennae_and_mouthparts       the sense-and-eat region
%     thorax        legs_and_wings                     the locomotion region
%     abdomen       digestion_and_reproduction         holds the main organs
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind what a region bears and recall the region:
%
%     ? insect_region_bears($Region, legs_and_wings)   % thorax
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three main body regions — `tail`, `wing`,
% `leg` — is deliberately NOT a row, so an insect-region recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a function. That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% region→feature pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `bears` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% feature / function the source EXPLICITLY states for that region — never one
% the source does not support. (Each atom is built only from words that appear
% in the source's own sentence for that region — "eyes", "antennae",
% "mouthparts" / "legs", "wings" / "digestion", "reproduction" — so every value
% stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every region→feature pair
% below was read out of a fetched University of Florida IFAS/EDIS entomology
% publication, and any pair whose feature could not be verified there would
% have been dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and
% the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   head → eyes_antennae_and_mouthparts
%     https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN758
%     (UF/IFAS EDIS ENY-1000/IN758, "Insects, Oh Yes!: An Introduction" —
%      University of Florida Entomology & Nematology Dept teaching resource)
%     "The head has three main parts: the eyes, the antennae, and the
%      mouthparts."
%
%   thorax → legs_and_wings
%     https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN758
%     (UF/IFAS EDIS ENY-1000/IN758 — University of Florida)
%     "Its main function is locomotion, since the legs and wings attach to the
%      body at the thorax."
%
%   abdomen → digestion_and_reproduction
%     https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN758
%     (UF/IFAS EDIS ENY-1000/IN758 — University of Florida)
%     "The abdomen holds all of the main organs. An insect uses its abdomen for
%      many different things. These include digestion, reproduction, defense,
%      and communication."
%
% (The three-region framing itself is stated on the same page — "it is easy to
% see the three body parts that make up an insect. The first region is the
% head. ... The second part of the insect is the thorax. ... the third body
% part is the abdomen" — another span of the same primary university source.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the UF/IFAS statement that fixes
% the first row (head → eyes_antennae_and_mouthparts). Every OTHER row's per-
% fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each value remains
% independently checkable. All sources are the UF/IFAS EDIS entomology
% publication, a University of Florida (land-grant Entomology & Nematology
% Dept) teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table insect_region_bears {
    columns region, bears

    row (head, eyes_antennae_and_mouthparts)
    row (thorax, legs_and_wings)
    row (abdomen, digestion_and_reproduction)

    source "The head has three main parts: the eyes, the antennae, and the mouthparts."
    locator "https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN758"
    trust authoritative
}
